
======================================================================================================
THE GRAVITOMAGNETIC (VECTOR) DRAG CORNER -- the last untouched dragged-frame escape
======================================================================================================
  a0 = c H_Lambda / Z, Z = sqrt(32 pi/3) = 5.78881 -> a0 = 9.3600e-11 m/s^2 (canonical);
  = (c/2) sqrt(G rho_Lambda). kappa = 1/2 is Carl's and is FITTED, not derived. Alt footing
  1.1300e-10. Note: nothing in this file depends on a0 -- which is itself worth saying,
  because it means the result is footing-free and cannot be moved by the footing fork.

======================================================================================================
S1. MAGNITUDE: how much does a GR gravitomagnetic field actually drag a frame?
======================================================================================================
  Linearised GR, h_0i = A_i, sourced by the matter CURRENT: laplacian A_i = -16 pi G rho v_i.
  For a mass M moving with V at distance r,  A_i ~ -4 (Phi/c^2)(V_i/c),  so the frame is
  dragged with velocity v_drag ~ 4 (Phi/c^2) V, i.e. a DRAG FRACTION
      f = v_drag / V ~ 4 Phi/c^2 ~ 4 v_c^2/c^2   (using Phi ~ v_c^2 for a flat rotation curve;
  that UNDERSTATES the potential depth by an O(few) factor, so f here is conservative-LOW,
  which is the direction that favours the hypothesis -- deliberately.)

    system                              v_c (km/s)     Phi/c^2  f = 4Phi/c^2  K needed = 1/f
    Milky Way at the Sun                     220.0  5.3852e-07    2.1541e-06      4.6423e+05
    SPARC bright end                         300.0  1.0014e-06    4.0055e-06      2.4965e+05
    SPARC median (Vflat=116.6)               116.6  1.5127e-07    6.0508e-07      1.6527e+06
    SPARC faint end                           20.0  4.4506e-09    1.7802e-08      5.6172e+07
  [OK  ] the GR drag fraction is 1.78e-08 to 4.01e-06 across real galaxies -- at least four orders below the f ~ 1 the door requires. The frame is essentially NOT dragged by gravitomagnetism
  [OK  ] MUTATION: a system with v_c = 0.5c gives f = 1.00 = O(1), so the estimator does produce order-unity dragging where dragging IS strong -- the smallness above is physics, not a broken formula

======================================================================================================
S2. *** THE MEASUREMENT THAT CLOSES IT: solar-system frame-dragging ***
======================================================================================================
  To reach f ~ 1 the gravitomagnetic coupling must be boosted by K = 1/f over its GR value.
  A UNIVERSAL boost is bounded where frame-dragging has been measured directly:
    Gravity Probe B (Everitt+ 2011 PRL 106:221101): -37.2 +/- 7.2 mas/yr vs GR -39.2
      => the coupling is confirmed to 18%, so |K - 1| <= 0.184
    LARES / LAGEOS (Ciufolini et al.): Lense-Thirring to ~2%
      => |K - 1| <= 0.020
    [both values FLAGGED as from memory, not re-fetched from the papers]

    bound                      K allowed  K needed (min)  K needed (max)              shortfall
    Gravity Probe B ~19%           1.184      2.4965e+05      5.6172e+07  2.11e+05 -  4.75e+07
    LARES/LAGEOS ~2%               1.020      2.4965e+05      5.6172e+07  2.45e+05 -  5.51e+07
  [OK  ] *** the boost the door needs exceeds the boost solar-system frame-dragging ALLOWS by 2.11e+05 at the very least favourable corner *** -- a MEASURED kill, not a budget proxy, and it does not depend on the RAR scatter, the dictionary exponent, or either a0 footing

  NOTE ON CONSERVATISM, so this is not read as a manufactured deficit: the sharper
  instrument would be the PPN preferred-frame parameter alpha_1, which governs exactly this
  kind of motion-relative-to-a-frame effect and is bounded near 1e-4 (LLR) to 1e-5 (pulsars).
  That would likely tighten the above by orders of magnitude -- but the mapping from a drag
  fraction to alpha_1 is NOT derived in this file, so it is NOT used. S2 stands on the
  direct frame-dragging measurements alone, which is the weaker and safer choice.

======================================================================================================
S3. THE 'TURN IT ON ONLY AT 1e2 kpc' ESCAPE FAILS STRUCTURALLY
======================================================================================================
  The corner was recorded as a vector drag at R_drag ~ 1e2 kpc, the hope being that a
  large-scale-only force evades solar-system bounds. It does not, and the reason is that
  Yukawa suppression works the WRONG WAY for this purpose:
      exp(-r/R) -> 1  as  r << R.
  A force of RANGE R is unsuppressed at every distance SHORTER than R. Long range means
  STRONG at short distance, not weak.

    range R             suppression at 1 AU    suppression at 10 kpc
    100 kpc                        1.000000                 0.904837
    1 Mpc                          1.000000                 0.990050
    10 kpc                         1.000000                 0.367879
  [OK  ] for every range at or above 10 kpc the suppression at 1 AU is below 1e-6, i.e. the force acts at FULL strength in the solar system -- so a long-range vector drag cannot hide from S2's measurements behind its range
  [OK  ] MUTATION: a range of 1000 km gives suppression 0.00e+00 at 1 AU, so the Yukawa factor DOES suppress when the range is short -- the test discriminates, and the failure above is specific to the LONG ranges this corner requires

  CONSEQUENCE: evading S2 requires SCREENING (a nonlinear, environment-dependent
  suppression), not range. That is S5.

======================================================================================================
S4. *** AN EXACT INVARIANCE THEOREM: uniform drag cannot remove the m=1 contamination ***
======================================================================================================
  The 32.2x cosmic-frame exclusion rests on the m=1 ORBITAL ASYMMETRY: with the frame not
  co-moving, a star's frame-relative speed varies around its orbit, and
      epsilon = V_pec / V_flat   (= 2.573 at V_pec = 300 km/s, SPARC median V_flat = 116.6).
  Ask what ANY uniform drag does to epsilon. Let the frame be a scalar multiple of the LOCAL
  matter velocity, u = f * v_local, with the disc matter co-moving with the star's orbit:
      v_local(phi) = V_bulk + v_orb(phi),   v_rel(phi) = |v_star - u| = (1-f)|V_bulk+v_orb|.

    v_rel(phi) = sqrt(V_b**2 - 2*V_b*v_o*sin(phi) + v_o**2)*Abs(f - 1)
    epsilon = (max - min)/(max + min) = (-sqrt(V_b**2 - 2*V_b*v_o + v_o**2) + sqrt(V_b**2 + 2*V_b*v_o + v_o**2))/(sqrt(V_b**2 - 2*V_b*v_o + v_o**2) + sqrt(V_b**2 + 2*V_b*v_o + v_o**2))
  [OK  ] *** d(epsilon)/df = 0 EXACTLY: the m=1 asymmetry is INDEPENDENT of the drag fraction *** -- (1-f) cancels between numerator and denominator, so NO amount of uniform drag, however boosted, removes the contamination the 32.2x exclusion rests on
    at V_pec = 300 km/s, V_flat = 116.6 km/s:  epsilon = 0.3887  (and it stays that for every f)

  NORMALISATION, stated so two different epsilons are not conflated: the corpus's
  epsilon = V_pec/V_flat = 2.573 is the SOURCE-level ratio; the 0.3887 above is the
  OBSERVABLE-level orbital asymmetry (max-min)/(max+min) of the same configuration. They are
  different normalisations of the same physics and must not be quoted interchangeably.
  The invariance is more general than either, and that is the real theorem:
  [OK  ] for GENERIC orbital phases phi_1, phi_2 the ratio v_rel(phi_1)/v_rel(phi_2) contains no f at all -- because v_rel is homogeneous of degree 1 in (1-f). So EVERY dimensionless statistic built from the frame-relative speed around an orbit is drag-invariant, whichever normalisation of epsilon is used. That is the theorem, and it is normalisation-free

  MUTATION CONTROLS -- both must CHANGE epsilon, or the theorem is vacuous:
  [OK  ] (a) the NON-LOCAL centre-of-mass frame u = V_bulk gives epsilon = 0 exactly -- so a frame that knows the galaxy's total momentum DOES remove the contamination. epsilon is therefore a discriminating statistic, and the theorem above is about LOCALITY, not about epsilon being unmovable
  [OK  ] (b) DIFFERENTIAL drag (f_t != f_r) gives d(epsilon)/d f_t != 0, so epsilon CAN be reduced when translation and rotation are dragged differently -- the theorem covers UNIFORM drag ONLY, and this escape is real

    the differential escape, priced: at f_t = 1 (full translational drag)
      epsilon -> 0   (zero for any f_r: the bulk contamination is gone)
      lambda = v_rel/v_orb -> Abs(f_r - 1)  = |1 - f_r|
    So differential drag with f_t = 1 and f_r ~ 0.5 would give epsilon = 0 and lambda = 0.5,
    costing only ~0.15 dex at p=1 -- INSIDE the 0.2232 dex budget. This escape is NOT closed
    by S4. It is closed only by S2: reaching f_t = 1 at all needs the forbidden boost.
  [OK  ] and that is stated against my own conclusion: with full translational drag the m=1 contamination vanishes identically (0), so S4 alone does NOT close the vector corner

======================================================================================================
S5. WHAT SURVIVES, NAMED AND NOT CLOSED
======================================================================================================
  The vector-drag corner is closed by S2 -- a measurement -- with one escape left standing:

   SCREENING. A chameleon/Vainshtein-like mechanism that suppresses the vector coupling in
   high-density or high-curvature environments (the solar system) and releases it at galactic
   radii would evade S2 without needing range (S3). Combined with DIFFERENTIAL drag (S4b),
   which can reach epsilon = 0 with lambda = O(1), this is a genuine two-ingredient escape.
   ITS COST, stated plainly: a new screening scale AND a new vector coupling AND a mechanism
   that drags translation differently from rotation -- three new ingredients to rescue one
   frame, against a framework whose entire appeal is that a0 = cH_Lambda/Z has ONE fitted
   number in it. That is a steep Occam price and it is not paid anywhere in the corpus.

  AND ONE THING THAT WOULD SETTLE IT CHEAPLY, not done here: derive the PPN alpha_1 induced
  by a drag fraction f. alpha_1 is bounded near 1e-4 (lunar laser ranging) to 1e-5 (pulsars)
  and governs exactly motion-relative-to-a-preferred-frame effects. If the mapping is
  alpha_1 ~ O(f), then even a SCREENED galactic drag with f ~ 1 is excluded by many orders,
  because screening suppresses the solar system but the pulsar bounds are galactic. That is
  the single computation that would close the corner outright, and it is one afternoon.
  [OK  ] S5 recorded against S2's measured shortfall of 2.11e+05, which is the load-bearing number and is independent of the RAR budget, the dictionary exponent, and both a0 footings

======================================================================================================
VERDICT
======================================================================================================
  THE VECTOR-DRAG CORNER CLOSES, and it closes on a MEASUREMENT rather than a budget:
   * GR gravitomagnetism drags the frame by f = 4 Phi/c^2 = 1.78e-08 to 4.01e-06 across
     real galaxies. The door needs f ~ 1, so a universal boost K ~ 1e5 - 1e8 is required.
   * Solar-system frame-dragging (Gravity Probe B ~19%, LARES/LAGEOS ~2%) allows |K-1| <=
     0.18 at best. SHORTFALL >= 2.11e+05. Footing-free, budget-free, dictionary-free.
   * The 'only at 1e2 kpc' escape fails structurally: Yukawa suppression is exp(-r/R) -> 1
     for r << R, so a 100 kpc-range force acts at FULL strength at 1 AU (5e-11 suppression).
     Long range means strong at short distance.
   * AND AN EXACT THEOREM WORTH KEEPING INDEPENDENTLY: if the frame is any scalar multiple of
     the LOCAL matter velocity, the m=1 orbital asymmetry epsilon is EXACTLY f-INDEPENDENT
     (d epsilon/d f = 0 identically) -- (1-f) cancels in the ratio. So uniform drag can never
     remove the contamination the 32.2x exclusion rests on, at any coupling strength. The
     non-local centre-of-mass frame CAN (epsilon = 0 exactly), which is why the surviving
     prescriptions are all non-local -- and non-local lands back on Route A, already priced
     at Carina 4.05 sigma and the Tr N = 1 leak.

  *** WHAT I AM NOT CLAIMING, and it runs against the verdict: *** S4's theorem covers UNIFORM
  drag ONLY. DIFFERENTIAL drag (translation and rotation dragged by different coefficients)
  reaches epsilon = 0 with lambda = |1 - f_r| = O(1), costing ~0.15 dex at p=1 -- INSIDE the
  budget. That escape is real and S4 does not close it; only S2's magnitude bound does. So the
  surviving escape is SCREENING plus differential drag: three new ingredients (screening
  scale, vector coupling, differential mechanism) to rescue one frame. Steep, unpaid, open.

  THE ONE COMPUTATION THAT WOULD CLOSE IT OUTRIGHT, named: the PPN alpha_1 induced by a drag
  fraction f. alpha_1 is bounded near 1e-4 (LLR) to 1e-5 (pulsars) and constrains exactly
  motion relative to a preferred frame -- and pulsar bounds are GALACTIC, so screening the
  solar system would not evade them. Not derived here; deliberately not used in S2.

  Prior art: Lense-Thirring 1918; Everitt et al. 2011 PRL 106:221101 (GP-B); Ciufolini et al.
  (LARES/LAGEOS) -- the two bound values are from memory, not re-fetched, and are flagged as
  such. NOT claimed: that a0 is derived; that the pincer is opened (Theorem 3 still forbids
  all local L, Theorem 8's argument mismatch stands); that the theory is closed.
